.TH POLO\-SESSION 7 "November 2026" "Polo @VERSION@" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
polo\-session \- the MATE (Polo) desktop session
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B MATE (Polo)
session logs you into MATE on Wayland with the Polo compositor in place of
Marco and
.BR mate\-session .
Display managers list it from
.IR /usr/share/wayland\-sessions/polo.desktop ,
which runs
.BR "polo \-\-session" ;
see
.BR polo (1).
.PP
.B polo
is the compositor, the window manager and the session manager at once.
It refuses to start if another program already owns
.B org.gnome.SessionManager
on the session bus.
.SS Environment
The session sets
.BR XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=MATE ,
.BR XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP=mate ,
.BR XDG_SESSION_TYPE=wayland ,
.BR DESKTOP_SESSION=mate ,
.B XDG_MENU_PREFIX=mate\-
and variables that make Qt and Firefox use Wayland.
It publishes these, with
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ,
.B DISPLAY
and a few others, to the D-Bus activation environment and to
.BR systemd (1)
\-\-user, and then starts
.IR polo\-session.target .
If the display manager did not start a session bus, the helper
.I /usr/bin/polo\-wayland.sh
starts one around Polo.
.SS Start-up
The session's programs start in the phases of mate-session: Initialization,
WindowManager, Panel, Desktop and Application, from the autostart entries in
.IR ~/.config/autostart ,
.I /usr/share/mate/autostart
and
.I /etc/xdg/autostart
(more precisely, the matching directories of the XDG data and configuration
paths).
Polo waits at most
.B phase\-timeout
seconds
.RB ( org.mate.polo.session ,
10 by default) for the programs of a phase before it starts the next.
The applications of the previous session are started again from
.IR ~/.config/mate\-session/saved\-session ,
as mate-session does.
If
.B restore\-window\-state
is set, their windows also return to where they were
.RI ( ~/.config/polo/saved\-windows ).
.PP
The programs that need the compositor's privileged Wayland interfaces
(the panel, for instance) are listed in
.BR trusted\-components ;
other programs do not get them.
.SS X11 programs
XWayland starts at once in a session.
Its windows are managed by Polo's own X window manager and follow the same
rules as Wayland windows.
.SS Settings
The session uses the dconf profile
.IR polo ,
installed as
.IR /etc/dconf/profile/polo ,
which adds the system database
.IR /etc/dconf/db/polo.d .
It turns off, and locks, the
.B mate\-settings\-daemon
plugins that Polo replaces on Wayland:
xrandr, keyboard, mouse, media-keys, a11y-keyboard, background and keybindings.
Packages run
.B dconf update
after installing it.
.PP
Everything else is configured through the usual MATE keys, including
.B org.mate.Marco.*
and
.BR org.mate.session ,
and Polo's own,
.BR org.mate.polo.* ;
see
.BR polo (1).
.SS Services
Polo provides on the session bus:
.TP
.B org.gnome.SessionManager
Logout, shutdown and inhibitors.
.TP
.B org.mate.Polo.Outputs1
Screen layout, for display settings programs.
.TP
.B org.mate.Polo.ScreenShare1
The screen sharing chooser.
.TP
.B org.freedesktop.a11y.Manager
The keyboard monitor interface of screen readers, answered only for Orca when
the screen reader is turned on.
.PP
For screen sharing and screenshots Polo starts
.B xdg\-desktop\-portal\-wlr
with its own configuration, and puts
.I /usr/share/polo/xdg
first in
.B XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
so that the portal frontend finds Polo's
.I mate\-portals.conf
(screenshots and screen casts to the wlr backend, everything else to GTK).
This changes nothing outside the session: the MATE X11 session is untouched.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/wayland\-sessions/polo.desktop
The session entry.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/polo\-session.target
Active while the session runs.
.TP
.I /etc/dconf/profile/polo
.TQ
.I /etc/dconf/db/polo.d/
The dconf profile and its database.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polo/xdg/xdg\-desktop\-portal/mate\-portals.conf
The session's portal configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mate/polo/monitors.xml
The saved screen layout.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The log goes to standard error, which is the journal under most display
managers, or to the file given with
.BR \-\-log .
A main loop that stops responding is logged with where it is stuck.
A crash is logged with a backtrace, and leaves a core dump for
.BR coredumpctl (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR polo (1),
.BR dconf (7),
.BR systemd.special (7)
